# Anserini Regressions: BEIR (v1.0.0) &mdash; NFCorpus

**Model**: [SlideOn](https://huggingface.co/lightonai/SlideOn) (using ONNX for on-the-fly query encoding)

This page describes regression experiments, integrated into Anserini's regression testing framework, using [SlideOn](https://huggingface.co/lightonai/SlideOn) on [BEIR (v1.0.0) &mdash; NCorpus](http://beir.ai/).
The model itself can be downloaded [here](https://huggingface.co/lightonai/SlideOn).

In these experiments, we are using ONNX to perform query encoding on the fly.
See [SlideOn ONNX inference](../../onnx-slideon.md) for model requirements and query settings.

The exact configurations for these regressions are stored in [this YAML file](${yaml}).
Note that this page is automatically generated from [this template](${template}) as part of Anserini's regression pipeline, so do not modify this page directly; modify the template instead and then run `bin/build.sh` to rebuild the documentation.

First convert the pre-encoded Parquet vectors to JSONL as described below.
From one of our Waterloo servers (e.g., `orca`), the following command will perform the complete regression, end to end:

```bash
bin/run.sh io.anserini.reproduce.ReproduceFromDocumentCollection --index --verify --search --config ${test_name}
```

## Preparing the Collection

On our Waterloo servers, the pre-encoded Parquet files are located at `/store/collections/beir-v1.0.0/slideon/nfcorpus`.
These files contain `id`, `indices`, and FP16 `weights`, rather than the JSONL records required by `JsonVectorCollection`.
Convert them using [to_anserini.py](../../../src/main/python/slideon/to_anserini.py), copied from the [upstream script](https://huggingface.co/datasets/lightonai/SlideOn-sparse-vectors/blob/main/to_anserini.py):

```bash
python -m pip install 'pyarrow>=21' numpy
python src/main/python/slideon/to_anserini.py \
  /store/collections/beir-v1.0.0/slideon/nfcorpus \
  collections/beir-v1.0.0/slideon-jsonl/nfcorpus/corpus.jsonl.gz \
  --k 512 --scale 100
```

## Indexing

Sample indexing command:

```bash
${index_cmds}
```

The important indexing options to note here are `-impact -pretokenized`: the first tells Anserini not to encode BM25 doclengths into Lucene's norms (which is the default) and the second option says not to apply any additional tokenization on the pre-encoded tokens.
For additional details, see explanation of [common indexing options](../../common-indexing-options.md).

